To use a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can add a few lines of code to your existing MATLAB script. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Load input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = fspecial('average', [3 3]);
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter);

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter using the `imfilter` function. The `fspecial` function is used to create a Gaussian kernel with a size of 3x3 pixels. The filtered image is then obtained by convolving the gray image with this kernel.

Next, we define the Sobel operators in the x and y directions as before. We then apply these operators to the filtered image using the `conv2` function. Finally, we combine the edges in both directions using the `sqrt` function and display the edge image using the `imshow` function.

This modified code will help reduce the noise in the input image before applying the Sobel operator, resulting in a more accurate and visually pleasing edge image.

