Your provided code is a good starting point for finding unique words in Text1 that don't appear in Text2. Here's an optimized version that can handle large amounts of text more efficiently:

```matlab
function unique_words = find_unique_words(text1, text2)
    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));

    % Remove stop words and punctuation marks
    stop_words = stopwords('english');
    punctuation = ['.' ',', '!', '?'];
    words1 = words1(~ismember(words1, stop_words) & ~ismember(words1, punctuation));
    words2 = words2(~ismember(words2, stop_words) & ~ismember(words2, punctuation));

    % Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
    unique_words = setdiff(words1, words2);
end
```

This modified function includes the following improvements:

1. Removes stop words and punctuation marks from the input texts before comparing them. This reduces the number of words to compare, leading to faster processing times.
2. Uses the `stopwords` function to define a list of stop words commonly found in English language. You can replace this with a custom list of stop words specific to your use case.
3. Uses the `punctuation` variable to define a list of common punctuation marks. You can add or remove punctuation marks as needed.
4. Returns the unique words as a output argument, rather than printing them directly. This allows you to further process or store the results as needed.

To use this function, simply call it with your two input texts:

```matlab
text1 = fileread('Text1.txt'); %read text1 from file
text2 = fileread('Text2.txt'); %read text2 from file
unique_words = find_unique_words(text1, text2);
```

The resulting `unique_words` variable will contain a cell array of unique words that appear in Text1 but not in Text2.

