When you add an attribute to a model in MVC, it affects both the model and the view. Here's how:

1. Model: The model represents the data and business logic of your application. When you add an attribute to a model, you're essentially adding a new property or field to that model. This new attribute can be used to store additional information about the data. For example, if you have a model for a customer, you might add an attribute called "phone number" to store the customer's phone number.
2. View: The view is responsible for displaying the data from the model to the user. When you add an attribute to a model, you need to update the view to reflect the new attribute. This can involve adding a new element to the view, such as a text input field or a label, to display the new attribute. For example, if you add a "phone number" attribute to a customer model, you might add a text input field to the view where the user can enter their phone number.

