To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `JwtSecurityToken` class and its `Payload` property. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;

public class JwtHelper
{
    public static string GetUsernameFromJwt(string jwtToken)
    {
        var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
        var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            IssuerSigningKey = key,
            ValidateIssuer = false,
            ValidateAudience = false,
            ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
        });

        return token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "username")?.Value;
    }
}
```

Replace `"your_secret_key"` with the secret key used to sign the JWT token. This method will return the username claim from the JWT token, or null if it doesn't exist.

To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` class and the `User` property. Here's an example:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer` package if you haven't already. You can install it using NuGet.
2. Next, add the following code to your `Startup.cs` file in the `ConfigureServices` method:
```csharp
services.AddAuthentication(options =>
{
    options.DefaultAuthenticateScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    options.DefaultSignInScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    options.DefaultChallengeScheme = JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
})
.AddCookie()
.AddJwtBearer(options =>
{
    options.TokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
    {
        ValidateIssuer = true,
        ValidateAudience = true,
        ValidateLifetime = true,
        ValidIssuer = "your-issuer",
        ValidAudience = "your-audience",
        IssuerSigningKey = "your-secret-key"
    };
});
```
Replace `"your-issuer"`, `"your-audience"` and `"your-secret-key"` with the appropriate values for your JWT token.
3. In your controller or service, you can now retrieve the username from the JWT token using the following code:
```csharp
var user = HttpContext.User;
string username = user.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
```
This will return the username from the first claim in the user's claims collection that has a type of `ClaimTypes.Name`. If there is no such claim, `username` will be null.

Note that this example assumes that the JWT token contains a claim with a type of `ClaimTypes.Name` that contains the username. If your JWT token has a different structure, you may need to modify the code accordingly.

To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package using NuGet.
2. In your controller or service, use the following code to extract the username from the JWT token:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;

public string GetUsernameFromToken(string token)
{
    var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
    var tokenDescriptor = new SecurityTokenDescriptor
    {
        TokenType = "JWT",
        Issuer = "your_issuer",
        Audience = "your_audience",
        SigningCredentials = new SigningCredentials(new SymmetricSecurityKey(key), SecurityAlgorithms.HmacSha256Signature)
    };
    var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token, tokenDescriptor);
    var userNameClaim = token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier);
    return userNameClaim?.Value;
}
```
Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your secret key, and `"your_issuer"` and `"your_audience"` with your issuer and audience.

3. Call the `GetUsernameFromToken` method with the JWT token you want to extract the username from:
```csharp
var token = "your_jwt_token";
var username = GetUsernameFromToken(token);
Console.WriteLine($"Username: {username}");
```
