To call a PowerShell script from within Terraform and allow for read-host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_input.ps1`) that prompts for user input using `Read-Host`.
2. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), define a `local-exec` provisioner that calls the PowerShell script. For example:
```hcl
resource "azurerm_resource_group" "example" {
  name     = "example-rg"
  location = "eastus"
}

resource "azurerm_virtual_machine" "example" {
  name                  = "example-vm"
  location               = azurerm_resource_group.example.location
  resource_group_name    = azurerm_resource_group.example.name
  network_interface_ids = [azurerm_network_interface.example.id]

  local-exec {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File read_input.ps1"
  }
}
```
This will run the `read_input.ps1` script when the virtual machine is created, prompting for user input. The output of the script will be displayed in the Terraform output.

Note that you may need to adjust the permissions on the PowerShell script to allow it to run successfully. Also, make sure to replace the `azurerm_resource_group`, `azurerm_virtual_machine`, and `azurerm_network_interface` resources with your own resource names and configurations.

