\begin{itemize}
    \item Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
    \item Premise 2: Socrates is human.
    \item Conclusion: Socrates is mortal.
\end{itemize}

This syllogism works by using the first premise to establish a general rule that all humans are mortal. The second premise then identifies Socrates as a specific instance of a human. Since the first premise states that all humans are mortal, it follows that Socrates, being a human, must also be mortal. This is a valid and logical conclusion based on the two premises provided.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
    \item Premise 2: Socrates is human.
    \item Conclusion: Therefore, Socrates is mortal.
\end{itemize}
This is a logical syllogism because the conclusion logically follows from the premises. The first premise states that all humans are mortal, which means they will eventually die. The second premise states that Socrates is human, which means he is part of the same group as all other humans. Therefore, according to the first premise, Socrates must also be mortal and will eventually die.

